# EmailJS credentials for the contact forms.
#
# Copy this file to .env and fill in the three values, then rebuild.
# .env is gitignored; this example is not, so the required keys stay documented.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Vite inlines anything prefixed VITE_ into the client bundle at BUILD time, so
# a value added after the build has no effect: always `npm run build` again.
# These three are not secrets in the usual sense -- EmailJS public keys are
# meant to be visible in the browser -- but they still belong out of git so the
# repo does not pin one account.
#
# Where to find them at https://dashboard.emailjs.com:
#   VITE_SERVICE_ID   Email Services   -> your service -> Service ID
#   VITE_TEMPLATE_ID  Email Templates  -> your template -> Template ID
#   VITE_PUBLIC_KEY   Account          -> General -> Public Key
#
# The template must use these three variables, because that is what both forms
# send (src/components/pages/Kontakt.jsx and src/components/pages/HireMe.jsx
# each send an object with these keys, folding the extra fields into message):
#
#   {{name}}     sender's name
#   {{email}}    sender's address, use it as the Reply-To
#   {{message}}  the message; HireMe folds service type and company into it
#
# Leaving these unset is what broke the forms for as long as the site has been
# live: emailjs.sendForm(undefined, undefined, ...) rejects, and until this was
# fixed the rejection was swallowed, so a failed send looked exactly like an
# untouched form.
#
# Two guards now exist because a build without these still exits 0:
#   npm run build   refuses to start (scripts/check-env.js)
#   npm run deploy  builds, deploys, then checks the live pages actually render
#                   a form (scripts/check-live.js), and a scheduled GitHub
#                   Action runs the same check every two hours.

VITE_SERVICE_ID=
VITE_TEMPLATE_ID=
VITE_PUBLIC_KEY=
